###### Abstract

We report on a discovery of a diffuse nebula containing a pointlike source in the southern blowout region of the Cygnus Loop supernova remnant, based on _Suzaku_ and _XMM-Newton_ observations. The X-ray spectra from the nebula and the pointlike source are well represented by an absorbed power-law model with photon indices of 2.2\\pm0.1 and 1.6\\pm0.2, respectively. The photon indices as well as the flux ratio of F_{\\rm nebula}/F_{\\rm pointlike}\\sim 4 lead us to propose that the system is a pulsar wind nebula, although pulsations have not yet been detected. If we attribute its origin to the Cygnus Loop supernova, then the 0.5-8 keV luminosity of the nebula is computed to be 2.1\\times 10^{31}\\,(d/540\\,{\\rm pc})^{2}\\,{\\rm ergs\\,s^{-1}}, where d is the distance to the Loop. This implies a spin-down loss-energy \\dot{E}\\sim 2.6\\times 10^{35}\\,(d/540\\,{\\rm pc})^{2}\\,{\\rm ergs\\,s^{-1}}. The location of the neutron star candidate, \\sim2{}^{\\circ} away from the geometric center of the Loop, implies a high transverse velocity of \\sim1850 (\\theta/2^{\\circ})\\,(d/540\\,{\\rm pc})\\,(t/10\\,{\\rm kyr})^{-1}\\,{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}}, assuming the currently accepted age of the Cygnus Loop.

